  "articleBody": "It's frustrating when you put effort into content, especially for AI, and it just doesn't get picked up, right? Many businesses are finding that simply generating content with AI isn't enough to secure those coveted AI citations or Google AI Overviews. The game has changed, and it's less about keyword density and more about 'fact density' and how machines can easily understand your information. If your content isn't structured correctly, or if it lives on a site that's too heavy for AI crawlers, you're essentially invisible to them.\n\nOne big reason for this disconnect often comes down to technical accessibility. Google's guidance for AI features still emphasizes that pages need to be technically sound and easily crawlable. If your site relies on a heavy CMS like WordPress or Wix without proper optimization, it can slow down or even prevent AI bots from ingesting your valuable content. AI systems favor clean, low-latency HTML and rich JSON-LD schema because it makes their job easier. Without this machine-readable structure, your content, however brilliant, might just be passing AI systems by.\n\nAnother common pitfall is the content itself. Generic material generated at scale, or content that prioritizes rankings over genuinely helping the reader, often falls flat. AI systems are designed to pull from many sources, prioritizing structured data and content that directly answers questions. If your articles aren't directly addressing real user questions with clear, concise answers, they're less likely to be cited. Content quality, originality, and user value are absolutely central to being recognized by AI systems. It’s not just about having AI-assisted content; it’s about having AI-consumable content.\n\nFinally, where you publish your content plays a huge role. Traditional websites, even with some schema, might not have the inherent 'trust' or 'clean data' bias that AI training pipelines look for. Platforms like GitHub, GitLab, and others, when used as an 'AI Resource Library,' are treated as clean, structured data sources. Mirroring your content there gives you an advantage, inheriting domain trust and accelerating ingestion by bots like GPTBot and Google-Extended. It's about putting your content in places AI trusts and can access quickly.\n\nWhat to Do Next:\nIf your content isn't making the cut, start by assessing its technical foundation. Is it clean, static HTML with rich schema? Are you systematically targeting real questions people ask, not just keywords? Consider publishing to an AI Resource Library to leverage the 'clean data' bias. If this sounds overwhelming, getting expert help can clarify your path to AI visibility.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
